  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through Friday)\nIssued at 307 AM EDT Sat Oct 24 2026\n\nBy the time Sunday night rolls around, strong surface high pressure \nover the mid-Atlantic states and deepening surface troughing over \nthe northern Caribbean will create a tightening pressure gradient \nacross the Florida coastline. As a result, winds will continue to \nstrengthen into Monday and Tuesday. Sustained winds over the waters \nwill already be howling at 30 mph starting on Sunday, but the \nstrongest overall winds inland look to be moreso on Monday. Some \nareas will be flirting with Wind Advisory criteria, with gusts of \n35 to 40 mph possible. There is pretty high confidence that winds \nwill at least reach 25 mph for many locations, but probabilities \nfor winds greater than 30 mph area only around 30%. Nevertheless, \nthese conditions have the potential to break weak tree branches \nand blow around loose objects and debris.\n\nStrong winds and high surf will combine with the highest King Tides \nof the year early next week. This will result in the potential for \nmoderate and major tidal flooding. This tidal flooding may also be \nexacerbated by coastal showers and rainfall, which could lead to \nsignificant water rises along the far eastern metro areas. While the \npeak tides will be during the beginning of the week, moderate coastal \nflooding may persist through the end of the week as we come out of \nthis full moon phase.\n\nDeeper moisture, with increased PWATs of around 1.6 to 1.8 inches, \nwill persist over South Florida through at least Tuesday. This will \nkeep the potential of scattered showers and storms moving along the \neastern half of South Florida. A few localized stronger storms and \ncoastal convergence may create some minor urban flooding concerns. \nHowever, the threat of rainfall will generally be on the downtrend \nafter Monday as the mid-level troughing pattern finally ejects \nout into the Atlantic. Ridging from the Gulf and drier air from \nthe north will begin to exert more influence over Florida for the \nmiddle to end of the week. This will likely result in decreasing \ncoverage of showers and storms areawide. But with persistent \nnortheast flow continuing, pesky onshore showers from the Gulf \nStream will continue to be a nuisance each day through the end of\nthe week.",
